WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.779
The SCP Foundation specializes in tracking
down and containing anomalies that are difficult

00:00:05.779 --> 00:00:11.410
to find, and that often will do anything they
can to avoid being found.

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:17.090
One anomaly in particular hates being seen
more than anything, and will punish anyone

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:20.380
who looks at its face with a horrific fate.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:26.929
SCP-096 is a mysterious creature, not only
because it destroys anyone who sees what it

00:00:26.929 --> 00:00:28.490
truly looks like.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:34.950
No one is entirely certain where it came from,
what exactly it is, or why it reacts so violently

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.240
to being perceived.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:42.140
There are, however, several theories as to
the Shy Guy's origin, and none of them are

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.520
particularly cheerful.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.970
Some suggest an experiment gone wrong, others
believe it to be the last remaining member

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:52.329
of an unknown species of mountain-dwelling
humanoids.

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:58.789
But one theory about SCP-096's origin stands
out as particularly tragic in nature.

00:00:58.789 --> 00:01:05.640
In this story, SCP-096 was not always a long-limbed,
pale beast cowering in the corner and hiding

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.400
its face from the world.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:12.390
It was once an ordinary man, like anyone you
might pass on the street.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:18.150
He was someone's son, someone's friend, a
person with hopes, dreams, and passions.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:25.440
A human being who, through anomalous circumstances
beyond his control, was made into a monster.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:28.340
Many years ago, there was a mountaineer named
Jack.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:33.299
All his adult life, he had been obsessed with
reaching higher and higher peaks, climbing

00:01:33.299 --> 00:01:38.720
every mountain he could in order to test his
limits, pit himself against the power of nature,

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:42.740
and bask in the sweet reward that was the
view from the top.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:48.710
It began as a sport, a way to build up strength
while taking in incredible natural beauty,

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:54.600
but over time as he conquered increasingly
difficult challenges, it became an obsession.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:59.380
Like every obsession eventually does, climbing
mountains began to take over his life, driving

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.060
everything else away.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:06.219
He struggled to hold down a job, his marriage
fell apart, and his relationships to his friends

00:02:06.219 --> 00:02:09.780
and family became increasingly distant and
strained.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.739
Still, he couldn't bring himself to care much.

00:02:12.739 --> 00:02:17.270
He spent his time alone in the wilderness,
or every so often embarking on trips with

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.750
other mountaineers.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:24.310
Whether he was going at it solo or working
with others, he was living for the climb.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:30.390
Over the years, he took on Cerro Torre in
Argentina, The Eiger in Switzerland, and Denali

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:31.390
in Alaska.

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:36.340
He especially enjoyed climbing in extreme
cold, braving the ice and snow as he embarked

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:37.780
on his climbs.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:42.220
The few fellow mountaineers he kept up with
gave him the nickname "Jack Frost."

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:46.900
After years of refining his craft, building
his strength, he set his sights on the highest

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:51.050
mountain in the world, his personal white
whale: Mt.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:52.720
Everest itself.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:57.090
It was one of the deadliest climbs in the
world, with its staggering height, storms,

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:01.200
and frozen graveyard of fallen climbers dotting
the snowy surface.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.220
But he was determined.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:07.900
He would not join the ranks of the fallen,
but instead, be one of the victorious that

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:11.890
reached the top of Everest and came back down
in one piece.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:14.310
He could feel it in his bones.

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:19.150
Jack arrived in Nepal with a pack full of
climbing equipment, the best boots money could

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:24.510
buy, and layers upon layers of clothing to
keep himself warm on the journey to the summit.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:29.970
He lowered his goggles over his eyes, summoned
his courage, and prepared for the incredibly

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.620
difficult climb.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:37.140
The cold seeped into his core, the winds nipped
at his face, numbing his nose and leeching

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:41.150
through his gloves and boots as he went, but
Jack pressed on.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:44.220
He welcomed the sensation, the bite of it.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:49.840
It was a reminder that he was still here,
that he was still alive.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.520
As long as he could feel the pain, he knew
he was alright.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.360
He knew that it was when the cold faded away,
turned to paradoxical feelings of warmth and

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:02.160
sleepiness, that a mountaineer was truly in
trouble.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.360
As he navigated the harsh mountain landscape,
Jack occasionally let his eyes drift off course,

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.250
taking in the sights around him.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:14.590
He could see shocks of blue, green, and red,
the distinct bright colors of boots, jackets,

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:18.660
and hats belonging to the fallen climbers
that came before him.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:23.470
Those who collapsed from exhaustion, who gave
in to altitude sickness or succumbed to the

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:24.470
cold.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:25.680
"Memento Mori."

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.729
He said softly to himself.

00:04:27.729 --> 00:04:33.389
These frozen bodies represented that ever-present
reminder of mortality, of death lurking just

00:04:33.389 --> 00:04:34.600
out of sight.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:36.789
It would not come for him though.

00:04:36.789 --> 00:04:39.150
Not today, not on this journey.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:40.570
He wouldn't let it.

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:45.009
The hours blurred together within the crunch
of the ice beneath his boots, the dig of climbing

00:04:45.009 --> 00:04:50.000
hooks into the earth, the tug of his trusty
ropes as he ascended ever higher.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.320
Jack came to a stop.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:55.670
There was no next step to take, nowhere higher
that he could go.

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:57.169
He had reached the summit.

00:04:57.169 --> 00:05:00.790
The white whale was captured, and Everest
was his.

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:05.729
For the very first time, he was truly on top
of the world.

00:05:05.729 --> 00:05:10.880
Now that he had made it to the highest point,
there was the all-important task of climbing

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:12.090
back down.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:16.639
At this altitude, he was still in the "death
zone," the place where human acclimatization

00:05:16.639 --> 00:05:18.660
was impossible.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:23.100
This was the tricky part, the point at which
even the most experienced of climbers might

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:28.040
get lost in the euphoria of the achievement
might let their attention slip.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:34.160
Any mistake during his descent, any misstep,
could be the thing that cost him his life.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:37.700
There was no room for error and no time to
waste.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:43.139
Just as Jack prepared to make his descent,
he saw a figure pulling itself over the ridge.

00:05:43.139 --> 00:05:45.790
From a distance, it looked like a fellow climber.

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:50.160
He noted the curious lack of equipment, and
the way the person seemed to be making their

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:55.130
way up the mountain with nothing but their
hands, but perhaps they had lost their pack

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:57.250
or left it behind for some reason.

00:05:57.250 --> 00:06:02.039
The figure was dressed in all white from their
hood to their boots, with a large pair of

00:06:02.039 --> 00:06:07.060
black mountain goggles covering what little
of their face wasn't concealed by the hood.

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:11.819
Uncertain if the figure was in need of assistance,
and surprised to see another soul on the mountain

00:06:11.819 --> 00:06:14.200
with him, Jack waved at the figure.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:15.220
"Everything okay?"

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:16.240
He called.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:20.070
There was no response, but the figure turned
to look at him.

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.610
At least, he thought it was looking at him.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:27.590
He couldn't see its eyes through the goggles,
but he felt the distinct sensation of it locking

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:30.389
its gaze with his, just the same.

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:35.099
As soon as the figure turned to look at him,
Jack began to feel strange.

00:06:35.099 --> 00:06:40.009
The pain, the sharp sensation of the cold
that he relied on to ground himself and remember

00:06:40.009 --> 00:06:44.110
he was really here, began to blur around the
edges.

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:49.280
The feeling softened, melting away along with
the ache in his muscles, the bleariness in

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:53.440
his eyes, the dry mouth that had nagged at
him for hours.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:58.470
He felt unbalanced, unmoored, a ship with
no anchor.

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:02.970
He couldn't explain why, but the sudden relief
from the cold made his chest tighten with

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:04.099
primal terror.

00:07:04.099 --> 00:07:08.260
He did not feel like one man encountering
another on the mountain.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:13.729
He felt the way he had years ago when he came
face to face with a mountain lion, staring

00:07:13.729 --> 00:07:18.849
down a predator and remembering all of a sudden
that humans do not naturally sit at the top

00:07:18.849 --> 00:07:20.670
of the food chain.

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:25.039
This figure, this thing...it was no man.

00:07:25.039 --> 00:07:27.470
It was no person.

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:31.849
Jack prepared to continue his descent, to
get away from the strange intruder as quickly

00:07:31.849 --> 00:07:35.330
as possible, but his feet would not obey him.

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:39.220
His eyes could not break contact with the
figure in white that was slowly beginning

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:43.259
to advance toward him, one slow step at a
time.

00:07:43.259 --> 00:07:44.450
It was in no hurry.

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:48.130
It knew he was not going anywhere any time
soon.

00:07:48.130 --> 00:07:53.220
He trembled as the figure drew closer, its
inscrutable face hidden beneath its hood and

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:54.570
behind its goggles.

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:59.610
He wished he could see its face, but dreaded
learning what secrets it was hiding.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:04.340
If only he could just unstick his feet from
the earth, break free of this spell that had

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:06.500
fallen over him, he could escape.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:12.590
But no matter how his muscles strained, how
he gritted his teeth with effort, he couldn't

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:13.630
get away.

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:19.319
As the entity came closer, the cold snapped
back into his body- but something was different.

00:08:19.319 --> 00:08:25.090
It was heightened, like every icy breeze and
freezing wind layered on top of itself, hardening

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:29.909
his muscles to rock, crystallizing the very
blood in his veins.

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:35.120
He felt as if his fingers, if he tried to
wriggle them, would snap off and fall to the

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:36.120
ground.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:40.849
His eyes thrummed in their sockets, his lungs
struggling to hold onto each breath as the

00:08:40.849 --> 00:08:44.300
cold constricted its fist around his chest.

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:49.860
He opened his mouth to speak but his voice
died in his throat, coming out in a weak whisper

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:50.860
of air.

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:55.740
Now, the thing was close enough to touch him,
and whatever force had been holding him still

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:57.540
and upright released him.

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:02.740
It was as if he were a marionette suspended
in the air, and the puppeteer released his

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:04.579
strings all at once.

00:09:04.579 --> 00:09:09.339
He crumpled to the snow-covered ground in
a heap, his cheek pressed into the earth,

00:09:09.339 --> 00:09:13.480
his eyes trained helplessly on the still-advancing
figure.

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:18.440
His lips formed the word "please..." though
he could not tell exactly what he was begging

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:19.440
for.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:20.459
His life?

00:09:20.459 --> 00:09:21.459
His freedom?

00:09:21.459 --> 00:09:23.540
A painless death?

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:28.370
He struggled to push himself to his feet,
but all the strength had gone out of his limbs.

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:32.370
He could barely lift his arm, let alone handle
the weight of his whole body.

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:37.500
The thing shaped like a man was right above
him, glowering down through those opaque goggles.

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:44.180
He still could not see its eyes, but somehow
he could feel the way it was looking at him.

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:49.850
Hatred was radiating off of it, disdain and
pure, unadulterated malice.

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:56.529
Then, it tilted its head to the side thoughtfully,
and Jack's mind was overwhelmed with images.

00:09:56.529 --> 00:10:01.660
He felt the phantom sensation of sunlight
on his skin, and springy green grass beneath

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:02.709
his bare feet.

00:10:02.709 --> 00:10:08.450
He could see, clear as day in his mind's eye,
a forest clearing in the middle of summer,

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:13.589
dewdrops sparkling on the petals of wildflowers
blowing in a gentle, sweet breeze.

00:10:13.589 --> 00:10:19.910
Then, he felt sand between his toes, smelled
the sea breeze, felt the comfort and contentment

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:21.110
of a nap on the beach.

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:27.900
It was not offering these things to him, but
something like them...something like peace.

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:30.570
It was offering him...a gift.

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:31.880
But he didn't want it.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:32.880
He couldn't take it.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:37.899
He was Jack Frost, dammit, he thrived in the
cold, the hardship.

00:10:37.899 --> 00:10:41.399
He didn't want some warm, sleepy fade out
of existence.

00:10:41.399 --> 00:10:46.339
If he went out, it would be on his terms,
doing the thing he had dedicated his life

00:10:46.339 --> 00:10:47.339
to.

00:10:47.339 --> 00:10:51.430
If he were going to lose his life, he wouldn't
roll over and just give it up.

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:54.910
It would need to be pried from his frozen
hands.

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:59.820
With every last bit of strength, he twisted
his head away from the entity's gaze, covering

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:01.800
his face with his hands.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:06.140
It grabbed at him, shaking his shoulders,
trying to force him to uncover his face and

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:09.430
meet its gaze once more, but he didn't budge.

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:10.570
He wouldn't.

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:11.570
He couldn't.

00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:16.850
Then, he felt the energy shift as it realized
he had refused the gift, that he would not

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:22.360
welcome the paradoxical feelings of heat and
security and slip away as it was asking him

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:23.360
to.

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:25.160
But it was not a victory.

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:31.520
He swore he could hear a voice in his mind
say, "Very well...you wish to be part of the

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:32.520
mountain?

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:36.260
I will make it so."

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:41.820
The world went white as Jack's eyes rolled
back in his head, his spine curving back until

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:42.820
it snapped.

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:48.280
His jaw cracked and distended, his arms and
legs stretched out long and twisted, his body

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:53.950
contorted at unnatural angles as the entity
began to rearrange his very being.

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:58.850
As the agony ripped through him, snapping
his bones and tearing his muscles, Jack kept

00:11:58.850 --> 00:12:01.060
his face cradled in his hands.

00:12:01.060 --> 00:12:06.490
He remained in that position even as he cried
his voice hoarse, as the pure fear and pain

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:13.200
overwhelmed his senses, until he forgot his
own name, forget he was ever a man at all.

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:18.430
When Jack was no more, when something else
sat there in his place, pale and shivering,

00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:24.269
body wracked with sobs, the King of the Mountain
turned and left him there, alone with his

00:12:24.269 --> 00:12:25.269
misery.

00:12:25.269 --> 00:12:27.900
Still, he would not uncover his face.

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:32.240
He would not let himself be vulnerable, would
not look up.

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:36.269
Days later, a group of mountaineers reached
the summit of Everest and spotted a hairless

00:12:36.269 --> 00:12:38.959
figure crouched in the snow, weeping softly.

00:12:38.959 --> 00:12:40.570
"Are you alright?"

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:41.610
One of them asked.

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:45.389
The figure lifted its head slowly, and locked
eyes with the strangers.

00:12:45.389 --> 00:12:52.690
Its eyes widened in horror, its mouth stretched
impossibly wide in a wordless scream of rage

00:12:52.690 --> 00:12:59.180
and hurt, and, with nothing human left in
its mind or heart, SCP-096 took its very first

00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:00.180
victim.

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:07.139
Now go check out “SCP-096 Shy Guy ESCAPE
- Incident 096-1-A Containment Breach” and

00:13:07.139 --> 00:13:09.750
“How Are Bodies Actually Frozen on Mount
Everest?

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:15.100
- SCP-1529 - King of the Mountain” for more
on the players in today’s harrowing video!

